<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Roaring exports lifted China=E2=80=99s eco=
nomic growth in the third quarter despite lacklustre consumer spending, whi=
ch lagged far behind factory output.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The country=E2=80=99s GDP grew by 4.5% yea=
r on year between June and September, better than economists had expected a=
nd up from the second quarter=E2=80=99s 4.3%. The economy grew by 4.6% in t=
he first nine months of the year, putting it on track to hit the government=
=E2=80=99s 2026 target of 4.5% to 5% growth without extra stimulus measures=
.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Industrial production rose 5.5% in Septemb=
er, up from 5.2%, while retail sales increased 1.1%, up from 0.4%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">However, fixed-asset investment fell 7.2% =
over the first nine months, property investment tumbled by 20.6% and infras=
tructure spending remained weak.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The analyst Stephen Innes said: =E2=80=9CT=
he headline GDP number tells you the economy is still moving forward; the i=
nternals tell you the factory floor is doing most of the running while hous=
eholds, property and investment are still somewhere behind looking for thei=
r shoes.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CContracting fixed investment and =
another deep property decline leaves the domestic economy badly out of step=
 with the factory sector.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CThe semiconductor boom is increas=
ingly a price story rather than a volume story.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CBeijing now has less urgency to e=
ase aggressively, but unless local demand catches up, China remains depende=
nt on the rest of the world absorbing what its factories produce, bringing =
the trade imbalance increasingly into view.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In Asian stock markets, Japan=E2=80=99s Ni=
kkei fell 0.6% while China=E2=80=99s CSI 300 rose 0.2%, Hong Kong=E2=80=99s=
 Hang Seng climbed 0.5% and South Korea=E2=80=99s Kospi added 1.5%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Increasingly violent street protests in Fr=
ance have put pressure on the euro, while French bonds sold off, pushing yi=
elds higher, ahead of a bond auction later in the day.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The French and Italian stock markets have =
tumbled more than 1%, while the sell-off in French government bonds deepene=
d.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">This has pushed the yield, or interest rat=
e, on the benchmark 10-year bond up by 7.5 basis points to 4.855%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The Stoxx 600 index of Europe=E2=80=99s le=
ading shares is down 0.4%. The UK=E2=80=99s FTSE 100 lost 0.6% or 66 points=
 to 10,815, while the German market slid 0.7%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The euro dipped 0.2% to $1.1218 versus the=
 dollar.</p>
